    "title": "The U.S. paid 2 cents an acre for this",
    "description": "In 1867 the United States bought Alaska from Russia for $7.2 million, about 2 cents an acre. Critics called it \"Seward's Folly\". Today Alaska is bigger than Texas, California and Montana combined, and from Little Diomede Island you can see Russia's Big Diomede, 2.4 miles away and on the other side of the International Date Line.\n\nSources: Encyclopaedia Britannica, \"Alaska Purchase\"; Council on Foreign Relations, \"Remembering the Alaska Purchase\"; NASA Earth Observatory, \"Yesterday and Tomorrow Islands\".\nImages: public domain via Wikimedia Commons (the 1868 Treasury warrant, Brady studio portrait of William H. Seward, Lomen Bros. photo of Nome in 1900, satellite image of the Diomede Islands). Maps: Natural Earth (public domain).\n\n#history #alaska #maps #shorts",
